body:
**Windows scope:** v0.4 supports the developer path through real WSL2 with Linux baha. A native Windows executable/host adapter and Windows containers are outside this release; build-only Windows evidence does not establish native runtime support.

Parent: #818

## Goal

Define and implement the Windows-side Host Platform semantics while explicitly separating **native Windows client behavior** from **WSL2 Linux execution behavior**.

## Required distinction

```text
Windows physical host
  └── WSL2 VM / Linux environment
        └── Docker/Podman/runtime workload

native Windows baha client
!=
baha running inside WSL2
```

Do not treat `GOOS=windows` and WSL2 as the same platform condition.

## Scope

### Native Windows

Evaluate and implement where applicable:

- platform identification;
- state/config/data directories;
- path/filesystem semantics;
- browser opening;
- terminal capability;
- trust-store capability;
- secure temporary/ephemeral material;
- self-update packaging capability;
- runtime discovery/classification.

Native Windows container/runtime lifecycle support is NOT implied.

### WSL2

When `baha` runs inside WSL2:

- identify WSL2 explicitly while preserving Linux execution semantics;
- use effective WSL2/runtime capacity rather than physical Windows RAM for mutation preflight;
- classify Docker Desktop integration vs Linux-local Docker/Podman honestly;
- define trust boundary: Linux trust inside WSL2 vs Windows browser/host trust;
- avoid assuming systemd unless it is actually enabled/available;
- preserve filesystem/path safety across Linux paths and mounted Windows paths;
- provide actionable diagnostics when a requested capability needs Windows-side action.

## Current-code relevance

- generic memory evidence currently reads Linux `/proc`; that may work inside WSL2 but must be classified as WSL2/effective-environment evidence rather than physical Windows evidence.
- browser opening already contains a Windows GOOS path.
- current self-update rejects non-Linux hosts.
- Linux trust code has no native Windows backend.
- XDG/home/state assumptions need explicit behavior for native Windows.

## Testing

### GitHub Actions

Use Windows-hosted runners for:

- native Windows build;
- unit tests that are truly portable;
- Host Platform Contract conformance;
- Windows path/state semantics;
- browser command selection via injectable execution;
- trust adapter unit tests without mutating runner-global trust unless safely isolated;
- packaging/self-update selection tests.

### WSL2

A Windows runner passing unit tests is **not** WSL2 acceptance.

Real WSL2 qualification requires a runner/environment that actually exposes WSL2. If GitHub-hosted runners cannot provide the required nested/WSL2 capability reliably, use a dedicated/self-hosted Windows runner later.

Record unsupported/untested status explicitly rather than simulating evidence.

## Acceptance

- [ ] native Windows and WSL2 are distinct platform classifications;
- [ ] native Windows contract conformance passes on Windows GitHub Actions;
- [ ] WSL2 detection and effective-resource semantics are covered by unit fixtures;
- [ ] real WSL2 smoke evidence is required before WSL2 is marked TESTED/SUPPORTED;
- [ ] no native Windows runtime support is claimed without real runtime evidence;
- [ ] failures tell the user whether the next action belongs inside WSL2 or on the Windows host;
- [ ] no Application/Runtime Provider contract changes are required.


## v0.4.25 mandatory WSL2 delivery acceptance (2026-10-09)

Parent #818 and release #174 now require a **working WSL2 developer journey**, beyond architecture qualification.
- [ ] `baha` installation/update inside real WSL2; supported Docker Desktop WSL integration or WSL2-local Linux Docker/Podman.
- [ ] End-to-end disposable Dev Local repo init/plan/up/status/doctor/logs/sql/cache/destroy, with clean owned-resource removal.
- [ ] Dev Remote enrollment/import and scoped Core mTLS on WSL2; host/browser trust separation and reconnection/rotation/offboarding.
- [ ] Detect real runtime VM memory/capacity and fail safely when capacity is insufficient.
- [ ] Negative tests for missing WSL distro/runtime, unavailable systemd, Windows-mounted repository paths, unsafe ownership and expired credentials.
- [ ] Runner evidence comes from actual WSL2 execution; native Windows CI or GOOS cross-compilation does not count.
Native Windows executable and Windows containers remain optional, not prerequisites for WSL2 support.


## Developer UX acceptance for WSL2

- [ ] A Windows developer completes installation and first application startup from one short public getting-started guide.
- [ ] Automatically detect WSL2, installed distribution, and usable Docker or Podman runtime; ask only for decisions that cannot be inferred safely.
- [ ] Explain missing WSL2, stopped runtime, memory limits, Windows-mounted repository paths, and browser/trust integration with a concrete next action.
- [ ] Dev Local works without mandatory BaseHarbor login; Dev Remote uses one-time guided profile import/enrollment and reuses the established session.
- [ ] The same canonical commands and wizard behavior work as on Linux; non-interactive CLI/JSON/MCP semantics remain consistent.
- [ ] An independent Windows developer completes install, app init, up, status/logs, app sql/cache, and owned destroy using only public documentation; record avoidable prompts and friction.


---

## Release assignment (2026-10-10)
This platform item now belongs to **v0.4.29 #876**, not the old v0.4.25 plan. Two sessions: Host/Linux/WSL2 (#819–#821) and Darwin/macOS + qualification (#822–#823); final audit #817. Host contract semantics must be pinned in v0.4.25 #872 and consumed here without silent redefinition; final release-wide acceptance #174 is v0.4.31. Real runtime evidence required.
